            "creation_time": "2026-11-19T15:13:57Z",
            "creator": "kevinushey",
            "count": 0,
            "is_markdown": true,
            "bug_id": 19342,
            "text": "## Summary\n\nHexadecimal constants with a binary exponent below -1023, such as `0x1p-1074`, are read as 0 by the parser, `as.numeric()` and `scan()`; since macOS's `%a` writes subnormals in that form, `deparse(x, control = \"exact\")` does not round-trip subnormal `x` there. Fixes a reproducible wrong result, for rare input.\n\n## Details\n\nThe hex branch of `R_strtod5()` (`src/main/util.c`) scales the mantissa by a factor 2^-expn built by repeated squaring of a `double`; for expn <= -1024 the factor overflows to `Inf` and the result is 0. PR#17199 fixed this only for strings with hex fraction digits. Seen in R 4.6.1; R-devel (r90937) has the same code.\n\nReproducible example: attached as `hex-subnormal-parse-repro.R`; it reads `\"0x1p-1024\"`, `\"0x1p-1074\"` and `\"0x1.54p-1068\"` as 0 instead of 2^-1024, 2^-1074 and `exp(-740)`, so its `stopifnot()` fails.\n\nProposed patch: attached as `hex-subnormal-parse.diff` (against r90937); it replaces the scaling and the PR#17199 special case with one `ldexpl()` call (`ldexp()` without `HAVE_LONG_DOUBLE`), which is exact and rounds once for subnormal results, and adds a test to `reg-tests-1e.R`. `make check` passes on macOS (arm64).\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/514, where CI results can be seen.",
